\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Kramers pairs at a self-opposite label}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let a self-adjoint operator #{H} on a finite complex Hermitian field space preserve the \vocabk{translation blocks}{fdip-016C}. Let #{A} be an antiunitary commuting with #{H} and satisfying #{A^2=-\bbI}. Suppose #{A} takes the block at #{r} to the one at #{-r} on the labels under consideration. At any such #{r=-r}, every spectral subspace for a single eigenvalue of #{H_r} has an orthonormal basis of pairs #{v,Av}. Its kernel, positive part and negative part therefore have even complex dimension.}

\proof{\p{Every eigenvalue is real. Its eigenspace inside the indicated block is stable under #{A}. For a unit vector #{v} there, antiunitarity gives #{\langle Av,A^2v\rangle=\cl{\langle v,Av\rangle}}. Substituting #{A^2v=-v} also gives its negative, so #{v} and #{Av} are orthogonal. Their plane is stable under #{A}, and antiunitarity preserves its orthogonal complement. Remove the plane and apply finite induction. An empty eigenspace has dimension zero. Adding the dimensions over positive or negative eigenvalues proves the dimension assertions.}}
